// Kiddo School — Garden Friends (School Garden) · Learning Path lessons.
// One individual flashcard teaching lesson per garden animal (owner doctrine:
// Learning Path = HOW to learn). Each lesson opens on its own /learn/{slug}/
// page: the original flashcard on the left, this step-by-step teaching content
// on the right, NO drawing canvas anywhere. Content is written fresh for
// teaching — the library card pages in data-garden-friends.mjs answer
// WHAT/WHY/PRACTISE and their paragraphs are deliberately not copied here.
// Age band: 2-year-olds — parent-led naming, gentle watching, sensory words.
// Every "notice" block sticks to what the card’s alt text verifiably states
// ("<Animal> picture card for toddlers"); no invented image details.
// Class number: src/curriculum.mjs puts the Age 2 band at Classes 9–14 and
// its garden class at 14 (Garden Bugs & Friends); these School Garden sets
// ride with that number, so every seoTitle ends "(School Garden)".
export const lpContent={
'bird':{
 title:'Bird',one:'Sit still for two minutes by a window and a bird will teach your toddler quietness, watching and a first wild friendship — free of charge.',
 seoTitle:'Bird Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Birds (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: turn the bird card into quiet window-watching, a seed-sprinkling game and the gentle first rule of nature — birds are for looking.',
 intro:'This lesson turns the bird card into your toddler’s first watching game: name the bird, notice it move, then keep still together and let a real one arrive on its own.',
 what:'A bird is an animal with feathers and wings that flies and sings. Birds visit gardens, hedges and windowsills every morning, looking for seeds and crumbs.',
 mean:'Birds give a two-year-old the day’s first magic trick: something appears, moves and disappears on its own schedule. Learning to watch without touching is the deepest thing the card teaches.',
 know:'The one big idea: birds come to the still and quiet. Running feet send them away. We look at birds, we talk softly to them, and our hands stay down.',
 why:'Watching a bird is a toddler’s first lesson in patience with a payoff — the stiller they sit, the longer the bird stays. That cause and effect is learned in the body, no words needed.',
 dev:'Attention and stillness (a real skill at two), first nature vocabulary, cause-and-effect noticing, pointing and naming, and the gentle beginnings of caring about living things.',
 vocab:'bird, feathers, wings, seeds, quiet, fly',
 teach:'1) Show the card and say the word bird slowly, twice — then make a little tweet together so the word arrives with a sound.\n2) Ask your toddler to find the bird’s beak and its wing on the card with one finger — pointing is how twos study a picture.\n3) Carry the card to a window and whisper: "maybe a bird is outside right now." Looking quietly is the game, and the whisper sets its mood.\n4) Sprinkle a few seeds on a sill or step, stand back, and count the seconds of stillness out loud — even ten quiet ones deserve applause.\n5) End with the rule, warm and short: birds are our look-at friends — hands down, feet still, and they come close all by themselves.',
 say:'"Here is the bird! Birds have wings and they fly. When we get quiet like this — ssh — and watch out the window, a bird might come and say hello. Birds are watch-friends — chasing only sends them away. Quiet feet find birds."',
 notice:'Find the bird on the card with one fingertip. Say bird together, then make a little tweet. Ask where the beak is and where the wing is — pointing is a two-year-old\u2019s way of studying a picture.',
 ask:'"What does the bird say?" "Can you point to the wing?" "Where did the bird go?" "Were your quiet feet still?" — questions a two-year-old can answer with a sound, a point or a giggle.',
 childSay:'"Tweet tweet!" "Bird gone!" Your toddler may press their nose to the window, freeze dramatically still for three whole seconds, or point at pigeons, crows and ducks on every walk afterwards, calling each one bird.',
 real:'Birds are the most reliable wildlife in any street: on the sill, in the hedge, strutting across the car park at the shop. Each sighting is a free rerun of the lesson — point, whisper, watch, wave goodbye.',
 play:'Seed sprinkle and freeze: shake a tiny cup of seeds onto a step, then play freeze together — crouch, hold hands tight, breathe loudly enough to hear, and wait. Whoever stays frozen longest wins, and the prize is usually a bird.',
 check:'Watch what happens at the next window: if your toddler drops to a whisper and points instead of banging on the glass, the lesson has landed — stillness was the whole point.',
 tips:'Keep one card by the window permanently. Two minutes is a full sitting at this age; stop while the pointing is still happy. Name the big obvious kinds as they come — black bird, big beak — precision can wait years.',
 safety:'Windows and doors stay shut while you watch together, and the sill stays for seeds, not elbows. If you feed birds, do it outside where they stay wild — and wash little hands after touching seed.'},
'frog':{
 title:'Frog',one:'Cushion lily pads, big frog jumps and one honest rule — the frog card turns your living room into a pond and your toddler into the springiest thing in it.',
 seoTitle:'Frog Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Frogs (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: hop like a frog from cushion lily pads, say ribbit, and meet the pond rule — frogs are watched from the bank, hands in pockets.',
 intro:'This lesson gets the wiggles out and the word in: your toddler becomes the frog, leaps across a cushion pond, and learns that real frogs live by water where grown-up eyes do the finding.',
 what:'A frog is a small green or brown animal with long back legs that hops high and swims well. Frogs live near ponds and puddles and catch bugs with a quick flick of the tongue.',
 mean:'The frog is the movement card of the set — jumping is what a two-year-old’s legs have been waiting to do. Naming the animal while being the animal is how the word sinks all the way in.',
 know:'Two things to know: frogs jump and say ribbit, and real frogs live down by the water where we go with a grown-up. We watch them from the bank; their skin stays untouched.',
 why:'Big jumps build balance, leg strength and landing control — genuine physical milestones dressed as a game. And the pond rule plants early respect: some animals are for looking, and that is not a loss, it is the friendship.',
 dev:'Gross motor power (crouch, launch, land), listening and responding ("jump!... freeze!"), animal vocabulary, and early self-control — the same legs that leap must also stop.',
 vocab:'frog, jump, pond, ribbit, lily pad, hop',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say frog, and crouch down low yourself — your toddler will copy your shape before they copy your word.\n2) Do one big jump together and land with a plop — then say ribbit! Linking word, jump and sound is the whole lesson in three seconds.\n3) Spread cushions across the floor — your lily pads — and hop between them, whispering ribbit on every landing to keep it playful, not wild.\n4) Tell the pond story simply: real frogs sit by the water, and we look at them from the bank with a grown-up, hands tucked away.\n5) Finish by crouching very small and still — a hiding frog — then one last huge jump to end on a laugh.',
 say:'"Can you be a frog? Crouch down low... and JUMP! Ribbit! Frogs have long strong legs and they live near the pond. When we see a real frog, we stand still and watch it — our hands stay in our pockets and our eyes do the finding."',
 notice:'Who is on the card? Say frog out loud, then crouch and do one big frog jump right there. Ask where the frog\u2019s eyes are, and finish with a ribbit — the picture, the jump and the sound make the whole introduction.',
 ask:'"Where does the frog live?" "Can you jump like the frog?" "What does the frog say?" "Do we touch the froggy at the pond, or only look?" — each one lands with a jump, a sound or a solemn little head-shake.',
 childSay:'"Ribbit!" "Froggy jump!" Expect cushion-leaping for days, frogs spotted in puddles that are actually shadows, and the proud announcement "frog see it!" after any walk near water.',
 real:'Frogs turn up after rain, at pond edges, even in window wells — and every spot-the-frog moment reruns the rule for free. Between sightings, puddles and bath time keep the leaping going.',
 play:'Lily-pad musical frogs: scatter cushions, play a hum or a song, and leap pad to pad. When the music stops, every frog crouches tiny and still. Freeze plus jump is the toddler combination hit.',
 check:'Ask for the sound and the move at a random moment tomorrow: if a crouch-jump-ribbit arrives unprompted, the word and the animal are glued together — lesson complete.',
 tips:'Soft landing spots only — rugs and cushions, never hard floors. Join every jump yourself; your leap is worth ten explanations. If a real frog is found, admire it, then steer away before small hands get ideas.',
 safety:'Leaping needs clear floor — slide the coffee table out of the flight path first. By real water, one grown-up holds one small hand at all times, and frogs are never picked up; their damp skin is delicate and ours is far too warm.'},
'squirrel':{
 title:'Squirrel',one:'Up the tree, down the tree, tail twitching — the squirrel card teaches your toddler to freeze, watch and follow the park’s best free show.',
 seoTitle:'Squirrel Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Squirrels (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: the squirrel card becomes a freeze-and-watch game, a nut-hunt under cups and a bushy-tail word your toddler will shout on every walk.',
 intro:'This lesson borrows the squirrel’s own game — dash, stop, look — and hands it to your toddler: scurry like a squirrel at home, then freeze and spot the real one from the path.',
 what:'A squirrel is a small furry animal with a big bushy tail that climbs trees and buries nuts to eat later. Squirrels dash along fences and branches all day long.',
 mean:'Squirrels are the only wild animal a toddler sees almost daily, doing something a toddler loves: hurrying. Naming the dash and the tail gives your child a script for every park visit from now on.',
 know:'The squirrel game has two moves: scurry fast, then freeze and look around — that is exactly how real squirrels behave. And the watching rule holds: we look at squirrels; our hands stay low.',
 why:'Freeze-and-watch is an attention exercise disguised as play, and spotting a squirrel in a tree is your toddler’s first genuine search-and-find. Owning an animal they see every day builds the confident feeling that the world is knowable.',
 dev:'Visual tracking (eyes following the dash), impulse control in miniature (freeze!), climbing-and-crouching movement play, vocabulary for tails and trees, and the pride of a spot announced loudly.',
 vocab:'squirrel, tail, tree, nuts, dash, climb',
 teach:'1) Hold up the card and say squirrel with a big wagging arm behind you — a make-believe tail makes the new word unforgettable.\n2) Play the squirrel’s own game indoors: scurry three steps, stop, look left, look right, scurry again — narrating each move as you both do it.\n3) Hide a toy nut under one of three cups, shuffle slowly, and let your toddler find it — squirrels always remember where their food is hiding.\n4) Take the card outside to any tree and whisper the question: is a squirrel up there right now? Eyes only, quiet feet.\n5) Close with the rule, spoken with a smile: squirrels are look-at friends — we watch their tails, and our hands stay by our sides.',
 say:'"Look — a squirrel! See the big fluffy tail? Squirrels run up the tree, stop, and look around. Can you scurry like a squirrel? Now freeze... and watch. We look at squirrels from far away, and they put on their show for us."',
 notice:'Look for the squirrel\u2019s tail on the card and wag an arm-tail of your own. Say squirrel twice, then ask what the tail is doing — bushy is the word you are fishing for with your toddler.',
 ask:'"Where is the squirrel going?" "What is its tail doing?" "Can you freeze like a squirrel?" "Did we touch the squirrel, or watch it?" — answered with a dash, a freeze and a proud point.',
 childSay:'"Squirrel!" shouted at full volume in every park. Expect arm-tail wagging at dinner, nut-hiding games under cushions, and the first honest freeze when a real squirrel crosses the path — the watching rule working exactly as built.',
 real:'Squirrels live wherever trees do: the garden, the park, the churchyard, the tree outside the bakery. Every dash between branches is a rerun — point, whisper, follow with your eyes only.',
 play:'Three-cup nut hunt: hide a nut (or any small toy) under one cup, shuffle, and let your squirrel find it. Swap jobs — being the hider is the cleverer half of the game.',
 check:'On the next walk, watch your toddler’s feet when a squirrel appears: still feet and a pointing finger mean the lesson landed — the freeze has replaced the chase.',
 tips:'Follow real squirrels with your eyes, never your feet — say that line on every sighting until your toddler finishes it for you. Keep the cup game tiny: three cups, one toy, big celebration.',
 safety:'Chasing is the danger here — a toddler dashing after a squirrel stops watching for paths and prams. Hold hands near roads, and let your fingers, not theirs, do any nut-handling; unknown nuts are not for mouths.'},
'rabbit':{
 title:'Rabbit',one:'Twitchy noses, soft hops and invisible carrots — the rabbit card turns your rug into a meadow and gives your toddler the gentlest animal word of all.',
 seoTitle:'Rabbit Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Rabbits (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: hop like a rabbit, twitch a nose, nibble an invisible carrot — and learn that real rabbits are watched kindly, never grabbed.',
 intro:'This lesson teaches the rabbit card with the whole body: hopping across the rug, wiggling a nose, then meeting the rule that real rabbits are soft to look at and careful with.',
 what:'A rabbit is a soft, gentle animal with long ears and strong back legs that hops. Rabbits live in burrows underground and munch grass and leaves.',
 mean:'Rabbits are usually the first animal a toddler names from books, so this card lands on friendly ground. The lesson’s real gift is gentleness: a big animal feeling practised on a small, shy creature.',
 know:'Rabbits hop, twitch their noses and have long listening ears. Wild rabbits are quick and shy — we watch from a step away, and even friendly-looking ones stay unpetted.',
 why:'Gentle is a skill, not a mood. Practising soft hands, quiet feet and a kind voice on the rabbit card builds the exact movements your toddler will need around pets, babies and other people’s things.',
 dev:'Gross motor (two-foot hops), body imitation (nose twitches are surprisingly hard and wonderfully funny), gentle-touch practice on a toy, and animal vocabulary with a feeling attached.',
 vocab:'rabbit, hop, ears, soft, nose, gentle',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say rabbit, and hop once — the word and the bounce should arrive as one event.\n2) Twitch your nose dramatically and wait: the copy may take a few tries, and the first successful twitch always earns a laugh.\n3) Hop a little rabbit trail across the room together, pausing at each stop to sniff the air like rabbits checking for danger.\n4) Hand your toddler a plush toy and practise the soft pat — one finger, slow and gentle — saying "gentle" as they do it.\n5) End with the rule in toddler words: real bunnies are watch-friends; we keep our hands to ourselves and let them hop away first.',
 say:'"A rabbit! Rabbits have long ears and they hop, hop, hop. Can you hop? Can you twitch your nose like this? When we see a real bunny, we stay quiet and soft — hands down — and watch it hop away. Gentle hands, gentle feet."',
 notice:'Say rabbit together, then hop once on the spot. Ask your toddler to point out the ears on the card — long ones — and finish by twitching your noses at each other like two rabbits in the rug-meadow.',
 ask:'"What does the bunny do?" "Can you hop two times?" "Where are its long ears?" "Do we touch the bunny or watch it?" — every answer arrives as a movement or a solemn nod, which is exactly right at two.',
 childSay:'"Hop hop!" "Bunny!" Expect spontaneous nose-twitching at breakfast, rabbits spotted in every field and storybook, and a soft one-finger pat on the cat or the teddy — the gentleness practice escaping into real life.',
 real:'Rabbits appear in gardens at dusk, at petting farms, in every second picture book. Each one is a rerun: name it, admire its ears, watch from a little distance, wave goodbye.',
 play:'Carrot patch: cut carrot shapes from orange paper, scatter them, and let your rabbit-hop toddler gather them into a basket — hopping to each one, nose twitching, big feet and quiet landings.',
 check:'Say "bunny" out of nowhere tonight: if a hop or a nose twitch comes back before the word does, the card did its work — the animal now lives in your toddler’s legs.',
 tips:'Practise the soft pat on toys daily; the hands learn faster than the rules do. If a real rabbit bolts away, treat it as the happy ending it is — "the bunny hopped home!" keeps watching sweet, never disappointing.',
 safety:'Hop on rugs, not tiles — hopping and hard floors collide. At farms, hands stay outside every hutch, and any animal touching is done to the plush toy only; real rabbits bite when startled and toddlers startle them.'},
'hedgehog':{
 title:'Hedgehog',one:'A pincushion with legs — the hedgehog card teaches curling up small, peeking out gently, and the clearest look-don’t-touch rule in the whole garden.',
 seoTitle:'Hedgehog Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Hedgehogs (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: curl into a hedgehog ball on the rug, peek out with just a nose, and learn why prickly garden friends stay safe in their own space.',
 intro:'This lesson turns the hedgehog card into a hiding game with a safety lesson folded inside: curl small, peek gently, and understand that some friends are loved from a distance.',
 what:'A hedgehog is a small animal whose back is covered in sharp spikes called spines. It snuffles around at night eating slugs and bugs, and curls into a ball when it feels unsafe.',
 mean:'The hedgehog carries the set’s clearest boundary: prickly means do-not-touch. For a two-year-old, meeting that boundary in a story-shaped way — curl small, peek out — makes the rule feel wise, not cruel.',
 know:'Two facts and one rule: hedgehogs have spines instead of soft fur, they come out at night, and we never touch them — the spikes are for protection, and we protect them right back by keeping our hands away.',
 why:'Toddlers learn some-things-are-not-for-hands from animals first, because the reason is visible — spines! A boundary with a visible reason is the easiest boundary for a two-year-old to accept and remember.',
 dev:'Body shaping (curling into a ball takes real control), gentle curiosity about other creatures’ bodies, the night-and-day idea, and early empathy: the hedgehog curls up because it feels safer, just like you sometimes.',
 vocab:'hedgehog, spikes, curl, night, snuffle, gentle',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say hedgehog, and run one fingertip very lightly along your own arm — prickly, prickly — so the spikes arrive as a feeling.\n2) Curl up on the rug into a tight ball together, then peek out with only a nose showing — the hedgehog\u2019s own hiding trick, and toddlers adore it.\n3) Snuffle around the room like hedgehogs hunting supper — soft snuffling noises, slow wandering, one imaginary slug eaten each.\n4) Explain the night habit simply: hedgehogs wake up when we go to bed, so a garden torch from the window is how we might ever see one.\n5) Land the rule warmly: hedgehogs are prickly, so our hands stay far away — we love them by leaving them be.',
 say:'"This is a hedgehog! Its back is full of tiny spikes — prickly, prickly! When it feels shy, it curls into a little ball, like this. Hedgehogs come out at night, and we never touch them. We look, we whisper hooray, and we let it snuffle on its way."',
 notice:'Find the hedgehog together and say its long name slowly — hedge-hog. Curl up small like the animal on the card, peek out with a nose, and ask what covers its back. Prickles is the answer worth waiting for.',
 ask:'"What does the hedgehog have on its back?" "Can you curl up small?" "When does the hedgehog wake up?" "Do our hands touch the hedgehog, or stay far away?" — answered with a curl, a whisper and a firm little head-shake.',
 childSay:'"Prickly!" "Hedgehog night-night!" Expect curl-up games at bedtime that double as wind-down, hedgehogs suspected under every bush, and the rule quoted back in emergencies — including, eventually, at you: "no touch!"',
 real:'Hedgehogs visit gardens after dark, snuffling along fences; most toddlers meet them in stories first, which is fine preparation. Any night sighting from a window becomes the day’s headline.',
 play:'Hedgehog hide-and-peek: curl up under a light blanket, peek out with a nose, hide again — then swap and let your hedgehog find YOU curled up. Slow, dark and cosy by design.',
 check:'Ask tomorrow what a hedgehog feels like: if the answer is prickly paired with pulling your hand back, both the word and the boundary have landed — the lesson is complete.',
 tips:'Let the curl-up game double as the calm-down trick on stormy days — a body that knows how to curl small can find calm on purpose. Never demo spines with real objects; the fingertip-on-arm pantomime carries the idea safely.',
 safety:'Real hedgehogs are strictly hands-off: spines scratch, and they can carry ticks and fleas. If a hedgehog calls by after dark, a shallow dish of water is the kind welcome — bread and milk make them ill, so those stay inside.'},
'worm':{
 title:'Worm',one:'The garden’s quiet underground helper — wiggle like a worm, find one after rain, and learn the tiny kindness of leaving it exactly where it works.',
 seoTitle:'Worm Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Worms (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: wiggle like a worm, meet the helper that turns leaves into soil, and learn the smallest kindness in the garden — worms stay where they dig.',
 intro:'This lesson makes the least glamorous card the most important one: your toddler learns the worm’s job, wiggles like one on the rug, and practises the gentlest feet in the garden.',
 what:'A worm is a long, thin, soft creature that lives in the soil. It eats old leaves and turns them into food for plants — the underground gardener, working all day in the dark.',
 mean:'Worms teach a two-year-old that important things can be quiet and hidden. No worm, no garden — and that idea, tiny as it seems, is the first brick of every ecosystem lesson your child will ever have.',
 know:'Worms wiggle and live underground; rain brings them up to the path. We look with our eyes and let them be — dry hands hurt their soft skin, so they stay where the wet ground keeps them.',
 why:'Watching a worm stretch and pull is mesmerising for a toddler and quietly teaches respect for small life. It is also the first time many children grasp that some animals have jobs — and the worm’s job feeds everything else.',
 dev:'Observation of tiny movements, new sensory words (wiggly, squishy, damp), kindness practise at its smallest and safest, and early cause-and-effect: leaves get eaten, plants get fed, garden grows.',
 vocab:'worm, wiggle, soil, rain, soft, garden',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say worm, and wiggle one arm like a wavy noodle — the word should come wiggling out of your mouth too.\n2) Lie on the rug and wiggle your whole bodies along the floor together, slow and stretchy, from one cushion to another.\n3) Tell the worm’s job in two sentences: it eats old leaves underground and turns them into plant food — the garden’s own tiny helper.\n4) After the next shower, go worm-spotting on the path — crouch low, hands clasped behind backs, and watch one stretch and pull.\n5) Close with the kindness: worms are soft and their skin likes the wet earth, so we look and walk on, leaving it to its work.',
 say:'"A worm! Wiggle, wiggle! Worms live down in the dark soil and munch old leaves — they help the flowers grow. After rain they come up to say hello. We look with our eyes, our hands stay down, and the worm keeps digging."',
 notice:'Say worm and wiggle an arm like a wavy noodle. Ask what the worm is doing on the card — wiggling somewhere, always — and let your toddler answer with a whole-body wiggle along the rug.',
 ask:'"Where does the worm live?" "Can you wiggle like a worm?" "What came before our walk — rain or sun?" "Do we touch the wormy, or watch?" — answered with a wiggle and a practised hands-behind-back.',
 childSay:'"Wiggle wiggle!" "Wormy go home!" Expect floor-wiggling races, worms announced at full volume after every shower, and the proudest hands-behind-back crouch you have ever seen at a puddle’s edge.',
 real:'Worms arrive after every rain, on paths, patios and driveways. Each sighting is a rerun of the whole lesson in ten seconds: name it, crouch, watch, walk on — kindness complete.',
 play:'Scarf worm: take a scarf or skipping rope and wiggle it along the floor like a worm on the move — then let your toddler hold one end while you hold the other, wiggling together in time.',
 check:'Watch the next rainy walk: if your toddler crouches, points and keeps both hands firmly behind their back, the watching rule and the worm both landed — no further lesson required.',
 tips:'Keep a mental note of damp worm corners near your home so a sighting is always a two-minute walk away. If the wiggle-game gets too silly for indoors, move it to the grass — wiggling was always an outdoor sport.',
 safety:'Worms are the safest animal on the card precisely because touching is off the table — their skin breathes, and dry little hands harm them. After worm-watching and any garden play, hands get washed before snacks.'},
'duck':{
 title:'Duck',one:'Waddle on your heels, quack at the pond, keep the bread in the bag — the duck card is the toddler classic, taught the kind way.',
 seoTitle:'Duck Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Ducks (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: waddle like a duck, quack from the bank, and learn the modern pond rule — ducks are watched and admired, and bread stays home.',
 intro:'This lesson takes the most-loved pond animal and gives it a body-and-voice routine: waddle, bill hands, quack — then the bank rule that keeps real ducks healthy and your toddler dry.',
 what:'A duck is a water bird with a flat bill and webbed feet that paddles on ponds and rivers. It dabbles tail-up in the water looking for plants and little water bugs.',
 mean:'Ducks are usually the wildest animal a two-year-old gets close to, which makes them the perfect practice ground for every watching rule — close enough to be thrilling, far enough to be safe.',
 know:'Ducks say quack and paddle with their feet; they stay on the water and we stay on the land. Bread is off the menu — it makes ducks ill — so we bring our eyes and our quacks instead.',
 why:'The pond edge is a natural boundary your toddler can see and feel: water here, bank there, ducks between. A rule with a visible line is the easiest kind for a two-year-old to keep — and quacking from the bank keeps the fun on the right side of it.',
 dev:'Gross motor comedy (waddling is a genuine balance challenge), sound imitation, the water-and-land idea, caring-from-a-distance behaviour, and first lessons in what different animal homes look like.',
 vocab:'duck, quack, pond, waddle, water, bill',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say duck, and put your hands together like a flat bill — opening and closing it as you say quack.\n2) Waddle across the room together, heels together, elbows out — the sillier the waddle, the faster the word sticks.\n3) Float an empty story: the duck dabbles tail-up for supper, then paddles off — act the dabbling with a bottom wiggle for maximum giggles.\n4) At a real pond, stand a step back from the edge, hold hands, and quack a hello to the ducks — that is the correct amount of close.\n5) Explain the bread rule on the walk home: ducks eat their own pond food, and sharing our bread makes their tummies poorly.',
 say:'"Quack, quack! A duck! Ducks paddle on the water with their flat feet and dabble for their dinner. Can you waddle across the room? At the pond we stand on the bank with me and call quack — the ducks stay in their water, we stay on ours."',
 notice:'Say duck, make bill-hands with your fingers, and quack. Ask your toddler to find the bill on the card, then waddle one slow circuit of the room together before anyone sits back down.',
 ask:'"What does the duck say?" "Can you waddle to the door and back?" "Where do ducks live — the water or the grass?" "Do we feed the ducks bread, or just say hello?" — answered with waddling, quacking and a serious little no.',
 childSay:'"Quack quack!" at every pond, puddle and bath. Expect bill-hands at dinner, waddle races down the hall, and ducks greeted by name from the buggy — with the bread rule quoted back at any grown-up who pulls out a sandwich.',
 real:'Ducks live on almost every pond, canal and river in town — the most reliable wild visit of all. Each one reruns the routine: waddle to the bank, hand held, quack, watch them dabble, wave goodbye.',
 play:'Pond crossing: lay a blue blanket as the pond, and waddle round its edge together — on the grass, never in the water — quacking at any ducks, socks or siblings found floating there.',
 check:'Ask at the next pond visit who feeds the ducks: if the answer is "nobody — they eat pond food," the rule took root. A waddle on request seals the lesson.',
 tips:'Bring a small bag of defrosted peas or corn if you must feed — but the quack-from-the-bank game is honestly enough. Keep the waddle on grass and rugs; pavements punish falling toddlers.',
 safety:'Pond edges are grown-up territory: one hand stays held the whole visit, prams parked behind you. Never let a toddler dangle feet or hands in duck water, and wash hands after any pond-side visit before snacks.'},
'turtle':{
 title:'Turtle',one:'The slowest animal in the garden is also the best teacher — the turtle card turns "slow" into a game your toddler can win without hurrying once.',
 seoTitle:'Turtle Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Turtles (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: a slow-motion race, a tuck-in-your-shell game and the turtle card’s big idea — everything arrives eventually, especially turtles.',
 intro:'This lesson celebrates the opposite of hurry: your toddler walks as slowly as they can, tucks into a shell and discovers that slow is a kind of clever.',
 what:'A turtle is an animal that carries its hard shell on its back and moves very slowly. The shell is its house — when it feels unsafe, its head and legs pull inside.',
 mean:'At two, everything is fast: legs, moods, days. The turtle card gives your child permission to try the other speed, and slowness turns out to be a skill they can practise and show off.',
 know:'Turtles carry their house everywhere and never rush. The game is to move slow... so slow — and the shell rule: when a turtle tucks in, it is asking for quiet, so we give it space.',
 why:'Slow-motion control is genuinely hard for toddlers and builds exactly the body regulation they will need for waiting, listening and sitting at preschool. Practising it as turtle play feels like play, not practice.',
 dev:'Motor control at both extremes (very slow walking after weeks of dashing), patience in seconds-long doses, the shell-as-home idea, and pretending — the first kind of imaginative play with a rule.',
 vocab:'turtle, slow, shell, house, wait, walk',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say turtle, and walk one exaggerated ultra-slow step — your toddler will match your pace before they match your word.\n2) Hold a slow-motion race: cross the whole room as slowly as possible; the loser is whoever speeds up, so dawdle shamelessly.\n3) Play shell: hug your own knees, tuck your head, then peek out — and let your toddler take a turn being the shell.\n4) Put a cushion on their back during a turtle walk and see if they can carry their house without tipping it — slow becomes necessary.\n5) End with the turtle fact as a lullaby-line: turtles carry their home everywhere, and they are never, ever late — turning up exactly when they turn up.',
 say:'"A turtle! Slow... so slow. The turtle carries its house on its back — hard and strong — and pulls its head in when it wants quiet. Can you walk like a turtle? Slower... slower... wonderful. The turtle is never in a hurry, and it always gets there."',
 notice:'Say turtle and ask where its house is — on its back, carried everywhere. Then walk one exaggerated slow-motion step so the card\u2019s speed becomes the room\u2019s speed. Slower... good.',
 ask:'"Where is the turtle’s house?" "Can you walk slow like a turtle?" "Is the turtle fast or slow?" "What does a turtle do when it feels shy?" — answered with a dawdle, a tuck and a whisper.',
 childSay:'"Sooo slow!" Expect slow-motion walking demanded at bedtime, cushions balanced on backs, and the shell game played under tables. "Turtle" may become your family’s word for taking it easy — that is a gift.',
 real:'Turtles show up at ponds, in books and at the pet shop window; tortoises in warm gardens count too. Every slow sighting is a rerun — and any queue, wait or long car ride is turtle-training time.',
 play:'Turtle school: crawl under a table or blanket with a cushion shell, carry it around a chair, and park it back on the rug without dropping it. Slow wins, always — that is the whole syllabus.',
 check:'Next time waiting is hard — a queue, a lift, dinner on the way — try "let us walk like a turtle there": if the pace drops and the mood lifts, the lesson is living in your toddler.',
 tips:'Grown-ups must genuinely lose the slow race now and then; nothing cements a game like beating the adult. Keep the cushion light and the shell brief — a minute of turtle is a full session at two.',
 safety:'Cushion shells stay light and never cover faces — no blankets over heads during shell play. Real turtles and tortoises are watched, not picked up; their beaks nip and their space is their own.'},
'mouse':{
 title:'Mouse',one:'Small, quick and gloriously shy — the mouse card teaches tiptoeing, squeaking and the good manners of letting shy things stay hidden.',
 seoTitle:'Mouse Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Mice (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: tiptoe like a mouse, squeak on request and learn the shy-animal rule — a mouse that is seen was never meant to be followed.',
 intro:'This lesson shrinks the whole body down to mouse-size: tiny steps, tiny squeaks, hiding behind the sofa — plus the respectful distance that keeps shy animals shy and safe.',
 what:'A mouse is a very small furry animal with big ears, a long thin tail and quick little feet. Mice hide in quiet corners and come out at dusk to look for crumbs and seeds.',
 mean:'The mouse is the set’s lesson in smallness — everything about it is a size word your toddler can act out with their body. It is also the set’s lesson in shyness: some creatures want to stay hidden, and letting them is the kind move.',
 know:'Mice are tiny, quick and shy; they squeak and tiptoe. If a real one is ever spotted, we stay put and watch for a second, then tell a grown-up — we never follow it into corners or cupboards.',
 why:'Tiptoeing is balance and control at its trickiest, and a toddler who can move quietly has mastered their body in a new register. The shy-animal rule also generalises beautifully: it is the same courtesy they will owe the cat, the baby and new friends.',
 dev:'Slow controlled movement (tiptoeing), whisper-voice practice, size vocabulary (tiny, little, small), understanding that animals have feelings about being seen, and dramatic hiding play.',
 vocab:'mouse, tiny, squeak, tiptoe, hide, whiskers',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say mouse, and make yourself small — crouched, hands up as tiny paws — before you say another word.\n2) Squeak together, loudly once and then in a proper mouse whisper; the contrast teaches quiet better than any shushing.\n3) Tiptoe across the room on a mouse mission — fetch one crumb (a block), carry it back under the table, and hide.\n4) Tell the dusk story: mice wake when the house goes quiet, which is why we nearly always see only their dash and their tail.\n5) Set the rule with care: if a real mouse ever runs past, our feet stay planted and our voice finds a grown-up — mice are shy, and shy means we let them be.',
 say:'"A little mouse! Squeak! Mice are tiny and quick, with whiskers and a long tail. They tiptoe out at night when everything is quiet. If we see one, we stay still and let it run home — shhh — a mouse likes to be hidden, and that is how we are kind to it."',
 notice:'Say mouse and make yourselves tiny — crouched small on the rug, hands up as paws. Ask where the whiskers are on the card, then squeak once, quietly, like a mouse that does not want to be found.',
 ask:'"Is the mouse big or tiny?" "What sound does a mouse make?" "Can you tiptoe like a mouse?" "If a real mouse runs by, do we chase it or stay still?" — answered with a squeak, a tiptoe and a firm little pause.',
 childSay:'"Squeak squeak!" Expect tiptoe missions to the kitchen, mice declared behind every cupboard, and sudden whisper-voice games. "Mouse!" may be announced at shadows — smile and agree to investigate slowly.',
 real:'Mice live near people everywhere — sheds, gardens, park corners at dusk. Real sightings are dashes; that is the whole show, and a fine one: point, gasp quietly, stay planted, tell the grown-up.',
 play:'Mouse house: drape a blanket over a chair as the hideout, tiptoe out to collect one block at a time, and stock the larder. A mouse that gathers is a mouse that practises quiet carrying, too.',
 check:'Play a random tiptoe dare tomorrow — "mouse-walk to the door": silent feet and a whispered squeak mean the lesson moved in for good.',
 tips:'Keep the squeak indoors and the whisper outdoors; the game doubles as your secret weapon for quiet transitions. If a real mouse appears indoors, stay calm and matter-of-fact — your toddler is reading your face first.',
 safety:'A startled wild mouse can nip, so the never-touch rule is absolute — and never follow one into a corner or cupboard. If mice move into the house, that is a grown-up job; cleaning up after them is not for little hands.'},
'mole':{
 title:'Mole',one:'Almost blind, brilliant at tunnels — the mole card takes your toddler underground with spoons, blankets and the best digging game in the set.',
 seoTitle:'Mole Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Moles (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: dig like a mole in the sandpit, crawl a blanket tunnel and meet the garden’s hidden tunneller who almost never comes up for air.',
 intro:'This lesson goes underground: your toddler digs, tunnels and crawls while learning that some garden neighbours spend their whole lives down below, and that is worth a whisper of wonder.',
 what:'A mole is a small animal with soft dark fur and big digging paws that lives almost its whole life in tunnels underground. It eats worms and bugs and rarely comes up to the surface.',
 mean:'The mole stretches a toddler’s world downward — there is a whole hidden layer under the lawn. Knowing a creature exists that you hardly ever see is an early lesson in how big the world really is.',
 know:'Moles dig tunnels with strong paws and live in the dark. Meeting one above ground is rare and special — if it ever happens, we whisper, we watch for a moment, and we let it dig on.',
 why:'Digging is deep sensory play — resistance, texture, mess — and tunnelling through blankets builds body awareness in tight spaces. Both are the kind of input busy toddler nervous systems are hungry for.',
 dev:'Hand and arm strength (digging, scooping, patting), spatial play (under, through, tunnel), sensory tolerance for the dark and cosy, and the abstract leap of imagining life underground.',
 vocab:'mole, dig, tunnel, underground, paws, dark',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say mole, and hold your hands up as digging paws — curl your fingers and show the scooping motion.\n2) Fill a pot with clean soil or head for the sandpit and dig with spoons and cups, narrating the tunnel as it grows longer.\n3) Build a blanket tunnel between two chairs and crawl through it, moles in the dark — a small torch adds real magic.\n4) Tell the truth simply: moles live down there all the time, and their eyes are tiny because the dark is their daytime.\n5) End with the molehill fact: those little soil volcanoes on lawns are mole work — a sign your underground neighbour is busy.',
 say:'"A mole! It digs, digs, digs under the garden — tunnels everywhere, all in the dark. Moles have big strong paws for digging. Can you dig a tunnel like the mole does? Can you crawl through it? If we ever see a real one, we whisper — it hardly ever comes up to visit."',
 notice:'Say mole and hold up curled digging paws. Ask where the mole lives — down below, in the dark — then scoop the air twice together. The card shows the tunneller; your hands show the job.',
 ask:'"Where does the mole live — up high or down low?" "Can you dig deep like the garden mole?" "Is it light or dark in the tunnel?" "What are those little molehills on the grass?" — answered with digging, crawling and a solemn "down low".',
 childSay:'"Dig dig!" Expect sandpit tunnelling marathons, blanket forts claimed as burrows, and molehills pointed out on every lawn walk. Dark-cosy play may become a bedtime favourite — moles sleep well.',
 real:'Moles stay hidden but their molehills advertise them on lawns, parks and verges everywhere. Spotting the hill and knowing who built it is your toddler’s first piece of detective natural history.',
 play:'Tunnel run: chair-blanket tunnel, crawl through, pat the "soil" (a duvet) flat behind you, then dig a spoon-pot mine in the sandpit and connect the two stories — the same mole, two playrooms.',
 check:'Ask what a mole does all day: "digging!" — with paws demonstrated — means the card moved in. Bonus if the answer arrives from inside a blanket tunnel.',
 tips:'Keep digging tools toddler-sized and the soil clean and chemical-free; a dedicated dirt pot beats the flowerbed for repeat play. Join the crawl — tunnels are twice as funny when the tunnel-leader fits badly.',
 safety:'Dig in sand, topsoil or a clean plant pot — never where chemicals, pets or unknown muck might be, and no soil in mouths, ever. Blanket tunnels stay low and open-ended so a crawl is always an easy exit; wash hands after all digging.'},
'lizard':{
 title:'Lizard',one:'Sunbathe, wait, zoom — the lizard card gives your toddler the freeze game to beat all freeze games, plus a sunny rock to call their own.',
 seoTitle:'Lizard Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Lizards (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: sunbathe like a lizard, freeze perfectly still, then scuttle away — with the sunny rule that quick little lizards are hands-off friends.',
 intro:'This lesson steals the lizard’s daily routine — warm up, stay still, dash — and turns it into a game your toddler can play on any mat, patio or patch of sunshine.',
 what:'A lizard is a small reptile with scaly skin, four quick legs and a long tail. It lies flat in sunny spots to warm up, then zips away at the first twitch of danger.',
 mean:'Lizards are the garden’s stillness champions, and stillness is a superpower a two-year-old can actually train. The dash afterwards makes the whole cycle irresistible: wait... wait... GO.',
 know:'Lizards sunbathe on warm rocks and paths to wake their bodies up, and they zoom off when startled. We watch from a step away; grabbing is how lizards lose their tails, and hands stay in pockets near them.',
 why:'Freeze-and-release play is impulse control with a joy at the end — the exact shape of self-regulation your toddler will use at preschool doors for years. And the tail fact lands the no-grabbing rule with a reason that sticks.',
 dev:'Stillness on purpose (a milestone at two), listening and responding to the "go" cue, warm-cool and sun-shade vocabulary, body imitation of a totally different body plan, and gentle curiosity about reptiles.',
 vocab:'lizard, sun, still, zoom, rock, tail',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say lizard, and lie down flat on the rug with your arms alongside your body — a sunbathing lizard is a funny sight and an easy copy.\n2) Hold the still together for a slow five-count, whispering warm... warm... warm, then shout fly! and scuttle across the room at full toddler speed.\n3) Play the cycle three times — sunbathe, freeze, zoom — so the order becomes the game and your toddler starts predicting the zoom.\n4) Tell the tail story with feeling: if someone grabs a lizard, the lizard leaves its tail behind and runs — which is why our hands stay tucked.\n5) Finish with a sunny-rock rest: lie flat on a mat by a real window and warm up quietly, lizards together, before the next zoom.',
 say:'"A lizard! It lies in the sun and gets warm — sss, so still. Can you be a lizard? Lie flat... be still... and when I say fly — ZOOM! Lizards are quick and shy. We watch them from a step away and our hands stay in our pockets, so their tails stay on."',
 notice:'Say lizard and lie down flat, arms tucked in, a sunbathing lizard on the rug. Hold still for a slow three-count, ask what the lizard is doing on its rock, then spring up and zoom once around.',
 ask:'"What is the lizard doing on the rock?" "Can you be still, still, still?" "What does the lizard do when you say fly?" "Do we touch the lizard, or watch it zoom?" — answered with stillness, a dash and pockets firmly filled.',
 childSay:'"Zoom!" Expect flat-on-the-floor sunbathing in odd corners, freeze-games demanded in queues and shops, and lizards spotted on every warm wall. The still-then-dash rhythm will become a family classic.',
 real:'Lizards bask on sunny rocks, walls and patios in warm places — and every glimpse is a fast, thrilling rerun. Even without a live one, warm pavements and sunny windowsills keep the story going.',
 play:'Lizard rock: designate a cushion as the sunny rock. Lizards must reach it, lie flat, and hold still until you whisper fly — then one lap of the room. Simple, repeatable, and it empties wiggles fast.',
 check:'Call "be a lizard" at a random moment: instant flat stillness followed by an explosive zoom means the lesson is fully installed — warm up, wait, go.',
 tips:'Count the stillness out loud and celebrate every extra second — the growing freeze is the progress chart. Keep the zoom zone clear of furniture corners; speed plus joy needs runway.',
 safety:'Stillness play happens on rugs and grass, never on roads or driveways. Real lizards are for eyes only — their tails drop when grabbed and wild reptiles can carry salmonella, so hands stay away and get washed after garden play.'},
'toad':{
 title:'Toad',one:'The frog’s warty cousin who walks instead of hops — the toad card slows the jumping game down and doubles the look-don’t-touch rule.',
 seoTitle:'Toad Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Toads (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: walk low and slow like a toad, compare toad and frog cards side by side, and learn that warty garden friends stay unhandled.',
 intro:'This lesson gives the toad its due: not every pond animal leaps. Your toddler learns the toad walk — low, heavy, slow — and the comparison game that sharpens both animal words at once.',
 what:'A toad is a pond animal with dry, bumpy skin and shorter legs than a frog. Instead of jumping high, toads walk and take small hops, and they like gardens as much as ponds.',
 mean:'The toad teaches contrast: same family, different body, different move. For a toddler, spotting how two almost-the-same things differ is a genuine thinking skill — and toad versus frog is the friendliest place to start.',
 know:'Toads walk low and slow on bumpy legs; frogs leap long on springy ones. Both live near water and both stay unhandled — toad skin is bumpy for a reason, and our hands stay off.',
 why:'Comparing two similar things is the toddler version of scientific thinking: notice, compare, name the difference. It also deepens the watching rule — learned twice, on two cards, it sticks twice as well.',
 dev:'Comparison and sorting skills, two new movement patterns (walk-low versus leap-long), descriptive vocabulary (bumpy, warty, slow, low), and the widening idea that garden animals come in related pairs.',
 vocab:'toad, warty, walk, low, hop, compare',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say toad, and crouch down low with heavy feet — a toad walk starts with the whole body dropped closer to the floor.\n2) Toad-walk across the rug: slow, low steps with little hops every few paces — different from the frog, and your knees will prove it.\n3) Lay the frog card beside the toad card and compare out loud: this one walks, that one jumps; this skin is bumpy, that one smooth.\n4) Act both animals back to back — leap like the frog, then drop low and walk like the toad — until your toddler switches on your call.\n5) Close the lesson with the shared rule: frogs and toads are both pond friends we watch only, and toads get extra space for their warty skin.',
 say:'"A toad! Its skin is bumpy — bumpy, bumpy! — and it walks low and slow, not hop-hop like the frog. Can you walk like a toad? Crouch low... heavy steps... good. Toads and frogs both live near the water, and both are look-at friends — hands behind our backs."',
 notice:'Say toad and crouch low with heavy feet. Ask whether this one hops high like the frog or walks low — the bumpy walker — then take two slow toad steps across the rug to settle the argument.',
 ask:'"Does the toad jump high or walk low?" "Which one has bumpy skin — toad or frog?" "Can you show me the toad walk?" "Where do toads like to live?" — answered with a crouch, a comparison and a point at the pond side of the room.',
 childSay:'"Toad walk!" Expect dramatic crouch-walking, spontaneous card-pairing on the rug, and toads declared on every damp evening stroll. Frog versus toad may become your toddler’s first debate topic.',
 real:'Toads turn up in gardens on damp evenings, under pots and logs, walking — always walking — somewhere. Each slow encounter is a rerun of the low walk and the hands-off rule in one go.',
 play:'Frog or toad? Call out one and your toddler does the move — high leaps or low walking. Swap roles so your toddler gets to be the caller; naming while directing cements both words.',
 check:'Line up both cards tomorrow and ask which one walks: a toad-shaped crouch is the answer you want — the difference has become your toddler’s own knowledge.',
 tips:'Keep the two cards near each other at home; pairs teach better than singletons. If you meet a real toad, admire its warts from a crouch and move along — the comparison game was always the destination.',
 safety:'Toad skin can irritate little hands, which makes look-only doubly right — no touching, no licking, no garden kisses, however funny the idea sounds. Wash hands after damp-garden play as standard.'},
'chipmunk':{
 title:'Chipmunk',one:'A squirrel with racing stripes and stuffed cheeks — the chipmunk card turns tidying-up into a gathering game your toddler will demand to play.',
 seoTitle:'Chipmunk Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Chipmunks (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: puff your cheeks like a chipmunk, scurry snacks into a basket burrow and learn why the stripiest garden visitor stays unheld.',
 intro:'This lesson uses the chipmunk’s best trick — cheeks crammed with food — as a game your toddler can win: gather fast, stash it away, and remember exactly where everything went.',
 what:'A chipmunk is a small striped animal, like a squirrel wearing racing stripes down its back. It stuffs seeds into its stretchy cheek pouches and carries them home to its burrow.',
 mean:'The chipmunk is the gather-and-hide specialist, and gathering is a game toddlers are brilliant at. It is also the rare animal lesson that ends with the playroom tidier than it started.',
 know:'Chipmunks carry food in their cheeks and hide it in burrows — and they remember every hiding spot. Wild and quick, they are a watch-only animal, even when the cheeks look funny.',
 why:'Gathering games build working memory (where does it go?), categorising (all the blocks here) and speed with control. And the stash-remembering trick is your toddler’s first memory challenge with a real payoff.',
 dev:'Carrying and stashing (whole-arm coordination), memory for hidden places, sorting by kind, fast-but-careful movement, and vocabulary for stripes, cheeks and burrows.',
 vocab:'chipmunk, cheeks, stripes, gather, burrow, seeds',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say chipmunk, and puff your own cheeks out big — hold a breath in each one — the picture and the puffed face land together.\n2) Point out the stripes: a chipmunk is a squirrel with racing stripes — then scurry once across the room to prove the speed.\n3) Set up the gather: scatter blocks or toy food on one side of the room, place a basket burrow on the other, and let your chipmunk ferry everything home, cheeks puffed.\n4) Hide one item under a cushion mid-game and ask where it went — chipmunks always remember their stash, and so will your toddler.\n5) Finish with the rule, cheeks still puffed: chipmunks are quick and wild, so we watch them scurry and keep our hands cheerful at our sides.',
 say:'"A chipmunk! See the stripes — a squirrel with racing stripes! It stuffs food in its big cheeks and carries it to its burrow, and it never forgets where it put things. Can you puff your cheeks? Can you gather like a chipmunk? We watch the little striped friend from far away."',
 notice:'Say chipmunk and puff both cheeks out wide. Ask your toddler to find the stripes running down its back, then scurry to the toy basket and back — one quick chipmunk run, cheeks held the whole way.',
 ask:'"What does the chipmunk carry in its cheeks?" "Where does it hide its food?" "Can you scurry like a chipmunk?" "Do we hold the chipmunk, or watch it?" — answered with puffed cheeks, a dash to the basket and a happy head-shake.',
 childSay:'"Cheeks!" Expect puffed-cheek faces at every meal, toys ferried to baskets in chipmunk style, and a suspicious talent for remembering where things were hidden. Tidying-up may never be the same again.',
 real:'Chipmunks live in woods, parks and gardens in many places, dashing between cover with cheekfuls. Every stripe-and-dash sighting reruns the lesson — point, puff, whisper, admire from the path.',
 play:'Cheek pouch relay: your toddler carries one item at a time pressed to their puffed cheeks from pile to burrow — one trip, one item, puff held the whole way. Silly, physical and weirdly calming.',
 check:'Ask tonight where the chipmunk keeps its food: "in its cheeks!" followed by a stash report of hidden toys means the word, the habit and the memory game all landed.',
 tips:'Turn the daily tidy into the gather game — chipmunks tidy the burrow, and the burrow is the playroom. Keep the stash-hiding simple: one item, one cushion, one triumphant discovery.',
 safety:'Wild chipmunks are quick, jittery and unholdable — hands stay down and snack-feeding is a no, for their diet and yours. If gathering real seeds or nuts outdoors, they go in the basket, not the mouth.'},
'raccoon':{
 title:'Raccoon',one:'Bandit mask, clever hands, night shift — the raccoon card turns hand-washing and water play into the cleverest game in the garden set.',
 seoTitle:'Raccoon Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Raccoons (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: wash toys like a raccoon, spot the black mask and learn why the night crew’s cleverest visitor stays strictly after-dark and hands-off.',
 intro:'This lesson borrows the raccoon’s famous habit — dunking and rubbing everything in water — and turns it into washing play your toddler can own, with the mask as the memory hook.',
 what:'A raccoon is a medium-sized animal with grey fur, a striped tail and a black mask of fur around its eyes. It has clever little hands and gives its snacks a famous wash in water before eating.',
 mean:'The raccoon gives your toddler two gifts: an unmistakable picture to name (the mask!) and a washing habit worth copying. Clever hands, it turns out, wash their food — and little hands can too.',
 know:'Raccoons wear a black mask, work at night, and dunk their snacks in water to give them a good rub. They are wild night visitors — clever hands or not, we never try to hold one.',
 why:'The wash-your-food story is a Trojan horse for hand-washing pride: if a raccoon washes everything, so can we. And the mask makes this the easiest animal on the whole set for a toddler to spot and name from a book.',
 dev:'Fine motor in water (rubbing, scrubbing, patting), the mask-as-identity idea (great for face vocabulary), night-and-day sorting, and hygiene habits smuggled in as animal play.',
 vocab:'raccoon, mask, night, wash, paws, clever',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say raccoon, and draw an imaginary mask around your own eyes with two fingers — the mask is the word’s best friend.\n2) Rub your hands together like a raccoon giving its food a wash — quick little scrubbing motions, and wait for the copy.\n3) Set a washing station: a shallow bowl of water, a few washable toys, and a towel — dunk, rub, pat, line up the clean ones like the raccoon would.\n4) Tell the night story: raccoons wake up when we go to bed, which is why the bin sometimes looks surprised in the morning.\n5) Land the rule kindly: raccoons are clever wild animals, so we watch them from windows and never reach out — wild hands and toddler hands do not mix.',
 say:'"A raccoon! See the black mask painted on its face — a little bandit! Raccoons have clever hands and they wash their food in water, rub-a-dub. Can you wash your toy like a raccoon? Raccoons come out at night. If one visits the garden, we watch from the window — clever hands stay wild."',
 notice:'Say raccoon and draw a pretend mask around your eyes with two fingers. Ask what is black on the raccoon\u2019s face, then rub your palms together like clever paws giving a toy its wash.',
 ask:'"What is black around the raccoon’s eyes?" "What does the raccoon do with its food?" "When is the raccoon awake — day or night?" "Do we touch the raccoon, or watch from far away?" — answered with mask-fingers, a scrub and a whisper.',
 childSay:'"Mask!" Expect finger-masks around eyes at random, toy-washing stations appearing at every puddle, and raccoons blamed for midnight noises. Hand-washing may gain a raccoon soundtrack — take the win.',
 real:'Raccoons prowl gardens, parks and bins after dark in many places; most toddler sightings are from windows, which is exactly right. Each masked face in a book reruns the lesson instantly.',
 play:'Raccoon wash day: line up three toys, dunk and rub each one in the bowl, dry them, and repeat until the water is much too busy. Add a washcloth for the table afterwards — the raccoon cleans up, too.',
 check:'Watch tonight’s hand-wash: if the rubbing comes with raccoon narrations, the habit and the animal are bonded — the lesson is complete and the sink is a Pond now.',
 tips:'Keep the washing bowl shallow and on a towel; raccoon play is wet play, and wet play needs a floor plan. Extend the mask idea to other faces — pandas, badgers — and watch the spotting skill generalise.',
 safety:'Raccoons can carry diseases and bite when cornered — window-watching is the whole friendship, and bin visits are grown-up business. After water play, empty the bowl and dry the floor so nobody skates.'},
'deer':{
 title:'Deer',one:'Tall, quiet and gone in a blink — the deer card teaches tiptoe wonder, swivel ears, and the big-animal rule that distance is respect.',
 seoTitle:'Deer Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Deer (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: tiptoe like a deer, freeze when the ears swivel, and learn the gentlest big-animal rule — deer are loved from far, far away.',
 intro:'This lesson brings the quiet bigness of the deer indoors: slow tiptoes, listening ears and a first understanding that the biggest garden neighbours need the biggest distance.',
 what:'A deer is a large, gentle animal with long legs, big ears and soft fur that lives in woods and fields. Deer hear everything and move away fast and quietly.',
 mean:'The deer stretches a toddler’s sense of scale — this neighbour is taller than you and still shy. Meeting bigness that needs space (not hugging) is an early lesson in how wild things stay well.',
 know:'Deer have big listening ears and long quiet legs. They are large wild animals: gentle to look at, never to approach. We watch from far away — through a window or from the path — and we speak in whispers.',
 why:'Distance is a hard idea for toddlers, who show love by reaching. The deer card gives distance a positive shape: the farther away you are, the longer the deer stays — watching becomes a game of patience with a giant prize.',
 dev:'Whisper-level quiet, listening attention (what did we hear?), scale words (big, tall, huge), self-restraint with a visible reward, and the idea that size and wildness travel together.',
 vocab:'deer, tall, ears, quiet, woods, far',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say deer, and hold a hand high above your head — bigger than you, bigger than me — so the size arrives before the details.\n2) Make deer ears with your hands and swivel them slowly: deer listen all the time, and so will we for this game.\n3) Tiptoe across the room, freeze at every creak, and listen — the deer walk is tiptoe plus stop plus listen, repeated.\n4) Explain the distance rule in toddler terms: deer stay happy when we stay far; if we tiptoe closer, they vanish — so the game is to be the stillness they trust.\n5) End by looking out of a window or across a field together and counting what a deer might hear from there — a bird, a car, your whisper.',
 say:'"A deer! So tall, so quiet. Deer have big ears that turn and listen — can you make deer ears? They live in the woods and they move away softly. When we see a deer, we stay far away and whisper — that is how we say we love you to a deer."',
 notice:'Say deer and hold a hand high — taller than you, taller than me. Make swivelling ear-hands together, ask what the deer is doing on the card, then drop to a whisper: deer talk is quiet talk.',
 ask:'"Is the deer big or little?" "What are its ears doing?" "Show me the deer tiptoe." "Do we walk close to the deer, or stay far away?" — answered with ear-hands, a tiptoe and a stretched-out "faaar".',
 childSay:'"Deer! Big one!" Expect ear-hands at bedtime, deer declared at the far end of parks, and sudden freezing mid-walk — ears up, listening. The whisper may spread to libraries and churches, courtesy of the deer.',
 real:'Deer appear at forest edges, parklands and safari-park windows, often at dawn or dusk. Each tall, quiet sighting is a rerun: freeze, whisper, count the seconds it stays — distance paying its dividend.',
 play:'Listening walk: indoors or out, tiptoe together and stop every few steps to name one sound you can both hear. Deer ears swivelling, toddler listening — attention training with antler-level dignity.',
 check:'At the next deer-in-a-book moment, watch the volume: if your toddler drops to a whisper and stays planted, the distance rule has become instinct — the lesson is complete.',
 tips:'Let the deer own the vocabulary of quiet: "deer feet" for tiptoeing past a sleeping baby is a household trick with a long shelf life. If a safari park is ever nearby, the window view is the field trip.',
 safety:'Deer are large wild animals — never approach, feed or chase one, however calm it looks; a startled deer is powerful and unpredictable. Keep toddlers inside fences and in hands at safari parks and forest edges.'},
'snake':{
 title:'Snake',one:'A long ribbon in the warm sun — the snake card teaches the clearest safety rule in the set, wrapped in a scarf-wiggling game toddlers love.',
 seoTitle:'Snake Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Snakes (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: wiggle a scarf snake along the floor, coil it up in the sun, and learn the one garden rule said plainly — we never touch snakes.',
 intro:'This lesson handles the garden’s most serious card with a calm voice and a playful prop: scarf snakes to wiggle and coil, plus the honest rule delivered without drama or fear.',
 what:'A snake is a long animal with no legs that slithers smoothly over the ground. Garden snakes lie in sunny spots to warm up and mostly want to be left alone.',
 mean:'The snake card is where the garden set gets serious, and toddlers can feel the shift. Handled calmly, it teaches the deepest safety lesson of all: some rules exist because the world has real edges.',
 know:'Snakes slither and like the sun. The rule comes in three moves: we never touch a snake, we step back slowly, and we tell a grown-up right away. Snakes are not nasty — they simply want their space, and we give it.',
 why:'A two-year-old who can name the rule and the move owns it. Fear is not the goal — respect is. Calm repetition of a clear rule beats alarm, and the scarf game keeps the snake friendly in imagination while the rule stays real.',
 dev:'Rule-learning with three clear steps, calm body response (step back, not scream), long-and-short vocabulary, scarf play for arm strength and coordination, and trust that grown-ups handle the scary bits.',
 vocab:'snake, slither, sun, long, back, tell',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say snake, and make a long sssss — long and slow, the sound the animal makes in every storybook.\n2) Draw the shape with your arm: a snake is one long curve with no legs — let your toddler trace it in the air with a finger.\n3) Grab a long scarf — your stand-in snake — and wriggle it over the floor together, then coil it in a warm circle for its nap.\n4) Say the rule in three beats, keeping your voice level and kind: see a snake — step back slowly — tell a grown-up. Practise the three beats like a little song.\n5) Close with calm: snakes live in the garden too, and they are happiest when we give them room — the scarf snake can live in the toy box, the real one stays outside.',
 say:'"A snake — sssss. It is long and it slithers on its belly, with no legs at all. Here is our snake rule, and it is an important one: we never touch snakes. If we ever see one, we step back slowly and we tell me. A snake\u2019s whole wish is to be left in peace — and peace is what we give it."',
 notice:'Say snake and stretch one arm into a long, slow curve — no legs anywhere. Make the sssss sound softly, ask what the snake is doing in the sun, and trace its long shape on the rug with one finger.',
 ask:'"What does the snake say?" "How many legs does a snake have?" "What do we do if we see a real snake?" "Who do we tell?" — the last two answered with the practised chant: step back, tell you.',
 childSay:'"Sssss!" "Step back, tell Mummy!" Expect scarf snakes everywhere, snakes audited in every picture book, and the rule recited with solemn pride. Calm respect — not fear — is the sound of this lesson working.',
 real:'Garden snakes sun themselves on warm paths, walls and woodpiles in many places. Every real sighting — rare and brief — is the rule performed exactly as rehearsed: back slowly, find the grown-up.',
 play:'Scarf snake trail: lay the scarf in wiggles along the floor and walk the line heel-to-toe like a tightrope, then coil it in a basket for its nap. Wiggles out, rule intact, snake tucked in.',
 check:'Ask casually what we do if we find a snake outside: the three-beat answer — back slowly, tell a grown-up, never touch — means the most important rule in the set is home.',
 tips:'Keep your voice flat and warm when the rule comes up; drama teaches fear where calm teaches competence. Retell the rule in new places — parks, holidays, grandma’s garden — so it travels like the scarf does.',
 safety:'This is the set’s real-deal safety card: never touch, reach for or corner any snake, and treat every unknown snake as a keep-away snake. If one appears often near your home, mention it to local wildlife services — not to your toddler.'},
'bat':{
 title:'The Bat',one:'The night sky’s own acrobat — the bat card flips bedtime upside down with hanging games, dusk-watching and wings that see with sound.',
 seoTitle:'The Bat Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Bats (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: hang like a bat at bedtime, flap through a darkened room and watch the evening sky — with the wild rule that bats stay wild.',
 intro:'This lesson turns the day on its head: while the rest of the garden goes to sleep, the bat wakes up. Your toddler hangs, flaps and peers at dusk for the garden’s night shift.',
 what:'A bat is a small furry animal with wings that flies at night. Bats twist and dart after insects in the dark and sleep through the day hanging upside down.',
 mean:'The bat introduces a deliciously strange idea: some neighbours keep opposite hours. Upside-down sleeping and flying by night make the bat the set’s wonder card — the one that says the world is bigger than my bedtime.',
 know:'Bats fly at night and hang upside down to sleep. They are wild animals that are never touched or carried — but watching their fast zigzags over the garden at dusk is a free airshow, and it is all ours.',
 why:'Dusk-watching is a wind-down ritual with a thrill in it: the sky empties of birds and fills with bats just as curtains close. A toddler who knows who flies at night watches the evening sky with ownership instead of sleepiness.',
 dev:'Body inversion play (bending to hang is a novel vestibular treat), day-night sorting, quiet evening attention, flapping large-movement play, and comfort with the dark — a bat is a friendly face for it.',
 vocab:'bat, night, wings, hang, fly, dark',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say bat, and flap your hands like small quick wings — bats flutter, fast and jittery, not slow and soaring.\n2) Do the bat hang: bend forward at the hips, arms dangling down, head low — a bat at rest. Hold it, wobble happily, then rise.\n3) Dim the lights and fly: flap around the room together as night bats hunting — a few laps is plenty; bats tire, and so do knees.\n4) Tell the upside-down fact with wonder: bats sleep with their feet holding on, heads pointing at the ground — every toddler’s favourite nonsense that is true.\n5) End at a window at dusk, if you can: look up, watch for fast flickers, and whisper bat! for each zigzag you spot together.',
 say:'"A bat! Night flyer — flap, flap, flap. When the sun goes down, the bat wakes up and catches bugs in the dark. And guess how it sleeps? Upside down, hanging by its toes! We watch bats from the ground and the window — they are wild flyers, and the sky is theirs."',
 notice:'Say bat and flap quick little hand-wings, fast and fluttery. Ask when the bat flies — at night, when we sleep — then bend forward and dangle like a bat at rest, toes holding on, head hanging low.',
 ask:'"When does the bat fly — day or night?" "Can you hang like a bat?" "How does a bat sleep?" "Do we touch the bat, or watch the sky?" — answered with a dangle, a gasp of delight and a point at the ceiling.',
 childSay:'"Bat! Night-night up!" Expect upside-down facts recited at dinner, bat-hangs off the sofa arm, and requests to check the sky before bed. The dusk vigil may become a treasured slow-down ritual.',
 real:'Bats flicker over gardens, parks and street lamps at dusk almost everywhere — a two-minute sky-watch usually finds one. Each zigzag reruns the lesson, and the window is the best seat in the house.',
 play:'Moths and bats: you be the slow moth wandering the room, and your bat chases with flapping hands — then swap. A soft "swoosh" sound makes the hunting official.',
 check:'Point at the darkening sky tomorrow evening without a word: if your toddler whispers "bat!" at the first flicker, the card has given your family a nightly wonder — lesson complete.',
 tips:'Keep the hanging game to sofas, beds and rugs — heads and floors negotiate poorly. If a real bat ever gets indoors, do not involve small hands: close the door, call for help, and save the story for breakfast.',
 safety:'Bats are strictly wild: never touch or pick one up, indoors or out — they can scratch and carry diseases, and only animal-carers should handle them. Dusk-watching happens behind glass or on a held hand, well away from open water and edges.'},
'owl':{
 title:'The Owl',one:'Big round eyes, silent wings, a hoot for bedtime — the owl card ends the garden day exactly where a toddler’s day ends: everyone else asleep, owl on duty.',
 seoTitle:'The Owl Lesson for 2-Year-Olds — How to Teach Garden Owls (School Garden)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for 2-year-olds: make big owl eyes, hoot softly in a dim room and learn who watches the garden all night — the perfect last card before lights out.',
 intro:'This lesson is built for the end of the day: wide owl eyes, two soft hoots, lights down low. The garden’s night guard becomes the friendliest possible bridge into sleep.',
 what:'An owl is a large night bird with big round eyes, silent wings and sharp talons. It watches and listens for mice in the dark and hoots to talk to other owls.',
 mean:'The owl closes the set with a comforting swap: when your toddler goes to bed, someone else takes the night shift. A garden with a night guard is a garden that is never truly empty — and that is a cozy thought, not a scary one.',
 know:'Owls doze through the daylight and do their hunting after dark, with huge eyes that see in the dark and a hoot that carries. We hoot softly, we look for them in books and windows, and we let them work their quiet shift.',
 why:'Bedtime needs rituals, and the owl lends its whole shape to one: eyes wide, two hoots, lights low. The night-bird idea also reframes darkness — the dark is when the owl is busy, and that makes the dark a workplace, not a monster.',
 dev:'Eye and face imitation (the owl stare is a genuine expressive milestone), soft-voice control (hoots only get quieter), day-night mastery, and a bedtime-specific calm that parents will reuse nightly.',
 vocab:'owl, night, eyes, hoot, wings, sleep',
 teach:'1) Show the card, say owl, and open your own eyes as wide as they will go — hold the stare, do not blink, and watch your toddler try to match it.\n2) Hoot together, twice and softly — hoo-hoo — cupping your mouth like a little horn; loud hoots are for another day.\n3) Turn your head slowly side to side, owl-style, without moving your shoulders — the slow swivel is irresistible to copy.\n4) Tell the night story in a low voice: when we sleep, the owl opens its big eyes and watches over the garden — quiet wings, soft hoots, all night long.\n5) End the lesson as a ritual: lights down, one wide-eyed look, two whispered hoots at each other — and off to sleep, while the owl takes the watch.',
 say:'"Hoo-hoo! The owl owns the night. Big round eyes, silent wings — it watches the garden while we sleep. Can you make your big owl eyes? Can you hoot softly? Hoo... hoo... When you are in bed, the owl is just starting its day — the garden is in good paws and wings."',
 notice:'Say owl and open your eyes as round as big coins — hold the stare and see who blinks first. Ask where the owl\u2019s big eyes are on the card, then hoot twice, soft as a goodnight. Hoo... hoo...',
 ask:'"When is the owl awake?" "What do its eyes look like?" "Can you give me a soft hoot?" "Who watches the garden at night?" — answered with a wide stare, a whisper-hoot and a snug little smile.',
 childSay:'"Hoo-hoo!" Expect wide-eye contests across the dinner table, hoots exchanged over the baby monitor, and owls located in every moonlit illustration. The hoot may become your household’s official goodnight.',
 real:'Owls hoot from tall trees and rooftops at night in many neighbourhoods; most toddlers meet them in books first, which suits the bedtime angle. A real evening hoot from a window is pure gold — whisper it back.',
 play:'Owl eyes contest: sit face to face in the dim light, open eyes wide, and see who blinks first — loser hoots. Calm, silly, and the finest pre-bed wind-down in this whole set.',
 check:'Tonight at lights-out, listen for it: two soft hoots from the cot mean the lesson has become the ritual — the owl now owns the hour before sleep.',
 tips:'Reserve this card for the last slot of any session; its power is the descent into quiet. Keep hoots whisper-level and the room dim — the owl works best as a lullaby with feathers.',
 safety:'Owls are wild hunters with real talons — admire them from windows, books and sanctuaries only, and never approach a perched or grounded owl. Any owl found on the ground needs a wildlife rescuer, not a rescuing toddler.'}
};
